<%# One pool: a single quiet line unless something is wrong with it. Spec §4.3-4.4.
    Locals: `pool`, `presenter` (HomePresenter), and optionally `orphan` (default false).

    `data-expanded` is the auto-expand rule: anything needing attention opens itself, so
    trouble is never hidden behind a chevron. Both states render server-side; the
    click-to-toggle interaction is Plan 2b, which is why there is no chevron yet.

    Status through `presenter.status_for`, never `pool.status` — PoolStatus defaults to
    Date.current and a bare call here would compute against a different day than the rest
    of the screen, silently.

    The row says only how the pool itself stands, in the §4.4 vocabulary. It does NOT
    repeat `pool_problem_label`'s "no account" wording the attention band uses: that band
    is a flat list where each row has to carry its own context, while here the group header
    already says where the money lives, and saying it again on every row would print the
    same sentence five times under a heading that just said it. %>
<% status = presenter.status_for(pool) %>
<% orphan = local_assigns.fetch(:orphan, false) %>
<%# An orphan is a problem whatever its own status says — nothing can fund it — so it opens
    with the rest of the trouble rather than reading as a quiet line under a loud heading. %>
<% problem = status.needs_attention? || orphan %>
<% rules = presenter.dated_rules_for(pool) %>
<div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0"
     data-pool-name="<%= pool.name %>"
     data-expanded="<%= problem %>">
  <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4">
    <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= pool.name %></span>
    <span class="text-sm <%= problem ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-500" %>">
      <%# `period_closed:` through the presenter, never a bare `pool.calculator` — same reason
          as `status` above: a calculator built here would date itself against Date.current
          and disagree with the rest of the screen silently. %>
      <%# `raised_after_distributing:` through the presenter for the same reason, one question
          further out: it compares the pool's rules against THIS period's latest distribution, and
          which period that is depends on `presenter.today`. Spec §8's rough edge — a pool flipping
          to `behind` because the rule was raised rather than because money went missing. %>
      <%= pool_status_label(status,
                            period_closed: presenter.period_closed?(pool),
                            raised_after_distributing: presenter.raised_after_distributing?(pool)) %>
      <%# The date only where the label has not already said one. Of the three quiet states —
          `on track`, `saving` and `left to spend` — only the first has a date to add: the
          other two have no anchored rule to take one from, so #due_on is nil there anyway. %>
      <% if status.due_on.present? && !status.needs_attention? %>
        · <%= status.due_on.strftime("%b %-d") %>
      <% end %>
    </span>
  </div>

  <% if problem %>
    <div class="mt-2 pl-4 space-y-1" data-role="pool-detail">
      <% rules.each do |budget, due_on| %>
        <div class="flex justify-between text-xs text-gray-600 gap-4">
          <span><%= pool_rule_label(budget) %></span>
          <span><%= number_to_currency(budget.amount) %> · <%= due_on.strftime("%b %-d") %></span>
        </div>
      <% end %>

      <%# Expanded with no dated rule to show is a real state, not a corner: an orphan
          savings goal may hold none at all, and a rate envelope goes :overdrawn on rules
          that never carry a date. An empty box under a red line would leave the loudest
          row on the screen as the only one explaining nothing.

          Three cases, not two. `rules.empty?` does NOT imply a rate rule exists: :overdrawn
          is guarded on the balance alone, so a pool with NO rules at all reaches it, expands,
          and used to promise the user it "refills at its rate each period" while nothing
          refills it — a flat untruth on the loudest row on the screen, and the exact shape
          this app's own seeds had. Each branch now says something true of what is there.

          `pool.budgets` rather than a count query: HomePresenter#all_pools eager-loads them. %>
      <% if rules.empty? %>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-600">
          <% if orphan %>
            No account holds this pool, so no distribution can reach it — give it one.
          <% elsif pool.budgets.any? %>
            No dated rules — this pool refills at its rate each period.
          <% else %>
            No rules on this pool — nothing funds it.
          <% end %>
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
